#chap-header("4", [Scene 3: The Recluse], "Persuading Sennakht to talk")

*Time budget: 25 minutes. Lesson: social play --- more than one approach
works, and who does the talking matters.*

The party returns to Keshto with evidence of methodical, scholarly soul
extraction. Merzaghaal hears them out, and his face gets progressively
harder to read.

#aloud[
  "Soul-work with laboratory discipline," Merzaghaal says slowly. "There
  is exactly one order that studies souls the way a gearsmith studies a
  mechanism, and its members do not take frontier holidays. But one of
  them lives here. Retired, she says. Sennakht. Keeps a hut past the
  tannery and speaks to nobody.

  "She has refused every request the Never-Still has ever made of her.
  Perhaps you will have better luck. Do not lie to her --- I am told she
  was a Lector of the Mortefactors for two hundred years, and I doubt she
  has forgotten how to read people."
]

== Sennakht

- *Race:* Human (Deathless --- she has looked sixty for a very long time)
- *Faction:* The Most Ancient and Noble Order of Mortefactors, formally
  retired
- *Personality:* Dry, precise, entirely unimpressed by weapons, ranks, or
  Black Guard contracts. She lives alone with her books, her preserved
  specimens, and a kettle that is always just off the boil. She does not
  want visitors. She especially does not want _these_ visitors, because
  she has already guessed why they have come.

#note[
  *Her secret, and why she resists:* Sennakht knows exactly who the
  culprit is. Šauhahten was her student, decades ago, before the Order
  expelled him for advocating soul experimentation --- and she is the one
  who taught him the foundations he built his heresy on. She retired out
  here, in part, to keep a quiet eye on the region's fey sites, and she
  has suspected for months that the disappearances were his work. She
  said nothing, because saying something meant admitting whose hands had
  shaped his.

  She is not an obstacle because she is hostile. She is an obstacle
  because she is ashamed.
]

#flavor[
  The hut past the tannery is small, scrupulously clean, and smells of
  paper and formaldehyde. Shelves line every wall: jars, bones, folios in
  a dozen dead scripts. The woman who opens the door looks the party up
  and down once, sighs like a scholar interrupted mid-sentence, and says:
  "You found the bodies, then. You had better come in before you say
  anything else where the neighbours can hear."
]

== The Negotiation

Sennakht will confirm what the party already knows almost immediately ---
the technique is Mortefactor-derived, and no, the Order did not sanction
it. What she will _not_ volunteer is the rest: the name, the place, and
the way in. Getting those three things out of her is the scene.

Run it as conversation, not as a dice gauntlet. Let players try
approaches in character, call for a check when an approach is genuinely
uncertain, and count roughly *two solid successes* (or one success plus
one honest, un-rolled human moment) as enough to open her up. Failures do
not end the scene --- they cost time and make her pricklier.

*Approaches that work:*

- *Influence D3 --- appeal to the victims.* Names help: Vazhan, Pel
  Torren, the row of bodies in watch colors. She has spent months not
  looking at this directly; make her look. (Mbazân's lane, Influence 7.)
- *Show the evidence --- no roll.* The notebook page from the cave. She
  reads the marginal note --- _"Their courage failed, not their
  theory"_ --- and her hands are not quite steady when she puts it down.
  She recognises the hand. This counts as a success, automatically.
- *Occultism D3 --- speak her language.* Demonstrate that the party
  actually understood what they saw: distillation of the animating
  principle, iterated circle revisions. She stops treating them like
  hired blades and starts treating them like colleagues.
- *Offer her the journals --- no roll, but a promise.* If the party
  offers to bring her Šauhahten's research journals --- so the Order's
  stolen work does not end up in worse hands, or simply so she can see
  how far he went --- she accepts on the spot. Whether they keep that
  promise in Scene 5 is up to them, and she will remember either way.
- *Impose D4 --- lean on her.* It works; she is old, alone, and
  practical. It also closes her like a book. She gives them the name, the
  place, and the price, in six flat sentences, and tells them never to
  come back. No tea, no warnings, no goodwill --- and she omits the
  advice about the door opening freely from the inside, which will cost
  the party a bad moment in Scene 5.

*If Kalariyar is in play:* Sennakht falters mid-sentence the first time
she looks at them properly.

#aloud[
  "A Shade. Here, of all --- " She catches herself, and for the first
  time her courtesy is not ironic. "The old sites will know you,
  night-child, even if you do not know them. Doors open differently for
  the Princess's hunters. Sit down. All of you, sit down."
]

#note[
  This is Kalariyar's hook paying off --- the fey sites really do
  remember Shades. Mechanically it functions as one automatic success in
  the negotiation. Narratively it seeds Scene 4: Kalariyar feels the
  shrine recognise them, whether or not they volunteer for the threshold.
]

== What She Knows

Once the party has her, deliver all of it --- this is the adventure's
central exposition, so give it room to breathe.

#aloud[
  "His name is Šauhahten. A Mirror. My student, once --- the best I ever
  had, which is precisely the problem. The Order expelled him for
  arguing, in an open lectorium, that souls could be rendered like fat.
  Prima Materia --- the seed-substance of alchemy. He believes it can
  restart the cycle of death and rebirth. He believes he is _healing the
  world_. Every corpse in your ravine is, to him, a regrettable line item.

  "You will find him beneath the old fey shrine, northeast, past your
  ravine --- where the resonance never faded. It never faded because it
  is not a ruin. It is a door, and it is sealed the way the Deep Court
  sealed all their doors.

  "The way is shut to the living. That is not a riddle --- it is the
  mechanism. The threshold opens for a death, freely given, inside its
  circle. A mortal who pays that price is simply gone. One of you ---"
  she looks around the room, unhurried, counting the Deathless, "---
  will die today, and get back up, and I suggest you decide which of you
  it will be before you are standing in front of the stones arguing.

  "He pays it too, you understand. Every time he goes home, he dies at
  his own door. He has done it dozens of times by now. Consider what
  that does to a person, and then stop expecting him to bargain like
  one."
]

- *The name and the man:* Šauhahten, Mirror, expelled Mortefactor,
  brilliant, certain, decades into his obsession. He is Deathless ---
  killing him is a setback for him, not an ending.
- *The place:* the old fey shrine northeast of Keshto, past the ravine.
  The laboratory is beneath it.
- *The way in:* a Deathless must die inside the threshold circle. The
  door takes the death; Essence takes them back. (She explains
  spot-revival in plain words if asked --- she has done it herself, and
  her contempt for the squeamish is gentle but total.)
- *Freely offered, unless she was Imposed upon:* the seal is one-way.
  From inside, the door opens for anyone. And: "Burn the laboratory. Not
  the man --- the _work_. The man will come back regardless. The work is
  what matters."

#note[
  *Keeping it to 25 minutes:* Sennakht wants, deep down, to be convinced
  --- do not make the players grind for it. If the clock is against you,
  she names her price immediately (the journals) and one success closes
  the deal.

  If the party never visits her at all --- possible, if they storm off
  --- Merzaghaal extracts the same information through faction channels
  overnight, minus the door's secret, and the party learns about the
  threshold the hard way: standing in front of sealed stones with
  Kalariyar's skin prickling. The adventure survives; the scene is just
  poorer for it.
]
